  "name": "FILARIA ANTIBODY DETECTION",
  "code": "1551",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p>The <strong data-start=\"98\" data-end=\"133\">Filaria Antibody Detection test</strong> is used to detect <strong data-start=\"152\" data-end=\"187\">antibodies (usually IgG or IgM)</strong> against <em data-start=\"196\" data-end=\"218\">Wuchereria bancrofti</em>, <em data-start=\"220\" data-end=\"235\">Brugia malayi</em>, or <em data-start=\"240\" data-end=\"255\">Brugia timori</em> &mdash; the parasites that cause <strong data-start=\"283\" data-end=\"307\">lymphatic filariasis</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Result</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Interpretation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Positive</strong></td>\n<td>Indicates <strong>exposure or infection</strong> with filarial parasites. May reflect an <strong>active</strong>, <strong>past</strong>, or <strong>latent</strong> infection. Further evaluation (e.g., microfilaria smear, antigen test, clinical signs) is needed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Negative</strong></td>\n<td>No detectable filarial antibodies. Suggests <strong>no current or past infection</strong>, or it is too early in the course of infection for antibodies to be present.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Equivocal/Borderline</strong></td>\n<td>Antibody levels are uncertain. Retesting after a few weeks or confirmatory testing is recommended.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-start=\"961\" data-end=\"993\"><strong data-start=\"965\" data-end=\"993\">Clinical Considerations:</strong></p>\n<ul data-start=\"995\" data-end=\"1417\">\n<li data-start=\"995\" data-end=\"1158\">\n<p data-start=\"997\" data-end=\"1158\">A <strong data-start=\"999\" data-end=\"1063\">positive antibody test alone does not confirm active disease</strong>, since antibodies may remain detectable for months or years after treatment or past infection.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1159\" data-end=\"1417\">\n<p data-start=\"1161\" data-end=\"1203\">Should be interpreted in conjunction with:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"1206\" data-end=\"1417\">\n<li data-start=\"1206\" data-end=\"1255\">\n<p data-start=\"1208\" data-end=\"1255\"><strong data-start=\"1208\" data-end=\"1226\">Clinical signs</strong>: e.g., lymphedema, hydrocele</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1258\" data-end=\"1303\">\n<p data-start=\"1260\" data-end=\"1303\"><strong data-start=\"1260\" data-end=\"1303\">Circulating filarial antigen (CFA) test</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1306\" data-end=\"1417\">\n<p data-start=\"1308\" data-end=\"1417\"><strong data-start=\"1308\" data-end=\"1362\">Microscopic examination of blood for microfilariae</strong> (especially at night for nocturnally periodic strains)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"1424\" data-end=\"1444\"><strong data-start=\"1428\" data-end=\"1444\">Limitations:</strong></p>\n<ul data-start=\"1446\" data-end=\"1671\">\n<li data-start=\"1446\" data-end=\"1561\">\n<p data-start=\"1448\" data-end=\"1561\">May show <strong data-start=\"1457\" data-end=\"1476\">false positives</strong> due to cross-reactivity with other parasitic infections (e.g., Onchocerca, Loa loa).</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1562\" data-end=\"1671\">\n<p data-start=\"1564\" data-end=\"1671\">Antibody tests are <strong data-start=\"1583\" data-end=\"1630\">not ideal for monitoring treatment response</strong>, since antibodies can persist long-term.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>",
